= January 1923

On 11 January, French and Belgian troops march into the Ruhr to seize coal for unpaid reparations. The Cuno government calls for passive resistance and pays for it by printing money. The mark is collapsing. Hunger, strikes and nationalist fury spread together.

For the <span class="p-kpd">Communist Party of Germany</span> this looks like the moment its founders waited for. The party has over two hundred thousand members, a daily paper, the *Rote Fahne*, and the backing of the <span class="p-comintern">Communist International</span> in Moscow.

You lead the party. Every month you decide what the <span class="p-kpd">KPD</span> does, and every decision strengthens some of its factions and angers others:

<span class="f-right">Brandler Right</span>: Heinrich Brandler and August Thalheimer, who hold the leadership. United front with the <span class="p-spd">SPD</span>, workers' governments in the states.

<span class="f-center">Middle Group</span>: Ernst Meyer, Hugo Eberlein, Wilhelm Pieck. Party unity above any line.

<span class="f-left">Fischer–Maslow Left</span>: Ruth Fischer and Arkadi Maslow in Berlin. No coalitions with reformists; prepare the offensive. Zinoviev's favourites.

<span class="f-ultra">Ultra-Left</span>: Karl Korsch, Werner Scholem, Arthur Rosenberg. Suspicious of the unions, of parliament, and increasingly of Moscow.

<span class="f-stalin">Thälmann circle</span>: Ernst Thälmann's Hamburg followers. In 1923 they are part of the Left, but they will follow whoever rules in Moscow.

Factions that grow strong can take the leadership at a congress. Factions that grow angry can break away. And Moscow can overturn whatever the German party decides.

This version covers the revolutionary year: January 1923 to the Frankfurt congress of April 1924.

- @rf_main: Begin.
